<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-08" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Center of Mass</title>

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <sidebyside widths="70% 30%">
        <p>
          A block of mass <m>M</m> lies on a frictionless surface, and a 
          particle of mass <m>m</m> is attached to it with a string that is 
          initially held horizontally. A long time after the string is 
          released, the system stops oscillating and the string hangs 
          vertically. At this time, the mass <m>M</m> is

          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Moving to the left/right (specify which)
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Static, to the left/right of where it started (specify which)
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Exactly where it started
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Depends on unstated assumptions (such as...)
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>

        </p>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>descr</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
              		\draw[very thick] (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(2,3)--(2,1)--(0,1)--(0,0);
		              \draw[ultra thick] (2,3)--(0.5,3);
                  \draw[fill=black] (0.5,3) circle (0.1) node[above] {$m$};
                  \node at (2.5,0.5) {$M$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

   <exercise>
    <p>
      An hourglass is made by dividing a cylinder into two identical halves, 
      separated by a small orifice. Initially, the top half is full of sand 
      and the bottom half is empty. The hourglass is placed on a scale, and 
      then the orifice is opened. The total weight of the hourglass and sand 
      is W. How does the scale reading compare to W right after the sand 
      starts falling, and right before the sand stops moving?   
    </p>
   </exercise>

   <exercise>
    <p>Find the position
    <m>
        (X_{CM},Y_{CM})
    </m>
    of the half-disk shown below, which has a total mass
    <m>
        M
    </m>
    and radius
    <m>
        R
    </m>
    . Its mass is uniformly distributed over the surface area. 
    If you are in Calc 3, find a partner also in Calc 3 and try to set-up a 
    double integral to calculate this. If you're not in Calc 3, find a partner
    also not in Calc 3 and read the instructions below.
    </p>

    <!--I used [scale=1.75] for this tikzpicture-->
    <image>
      <shortdescription>descr</shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \draw[ultra thick] (-2,0)--(2,0);
          \draw[ultra thick] (2,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=180, radius=2];

          \begin{scope}[xshift=6cm]
          \draw[ultra thick] (-2,0)--(2,0);
          \draw[ultra thick] (2,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=180, radius=2];
          \draw[red,|-|,very thick](1,0)--(1,1) node[pos=0.6,right] {$y$};
          \draw[red,|-|,very thick](0.75,0.75)--(0.75,1) node[pos=0.6,left] {$dy$};
          \foreach \x in {0.25,0.5,0.75,1,1.25,1.5,1.75}
            \draw (-{sqrt(4-\x*\x)},\x)--({sqrt(4-\x*\x)},\x);
          \end{scope}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                First things first, what is <m>X_{CM}</m>?
                Do we have to go through the trouble of calculating it?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                To find <m>Y_{CM}</m>, we will divide the semicircle into thin
                strips of height <m>dy</m>, as shown in the drawing.
                We will label the position of each strip with the coordinate
                <m>y</m>.
                What is the width of the strip at height <m>y</m>?
                Hint: Pythagoras, and remember that <m>dy</m> is eventually going to
                go to 0, so don't get too hung-up things like ''what do I do about
                the different widths?''
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                How much mass does this strip have?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                What is the contribution <m>dY_{CM}</m> of this strip to the
                position <m>Y_{CM}</m>?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Add up all the <m>dY_{CM}</m> by calculating the appropriate
                integral (with limits!).
                Ans: <m>~{Y_{CM}=\dfrac{4R}{3\pi}}</m>
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
   </exercise>
</worksheet>
